<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 9 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">The Story of Writing</h1><p class="path-tagline">From wedges pressed into clay to the printed page: how the world learned to write.</p><p class="path-intro">Writing was invented more than once, and borrowed far more often. It began in Mesopotamia and Egypt with temple accounts and royal display, and from those beginnings scripts were adapted and reinvented across Eurasia: syllables for the Mycenaean palaces, an alphabet pressed into clay at Ugarit, letters made for the Slavs and for the people of Korea. This path follows that story from the first clay tablets to the printers who multiplied the written word, with the decipherers who taught us to read the old scripts again.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Thinker</a> · <a href="/start">the Builder</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cuneiform.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Term</span><span class="path-step-name">Cuneiform</span><span class="path-step-why">Wedge-shaped signs pressed into clay, invented in Mesopotamia in the late fourth millennium BCE and used for some three thousand years, for Sumerian, Akkadian, Hittite and more.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/egyptian-hieroglyphs"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/egyptian-hieroglyphs.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Term</span><span class="path-step-name">Egyptian hieroglyphs</span><span class="path-step-why">Egypt's formal script of pictorial signs for sounds and words: the peace treaty after Kadesh survives in them at Karnak, and in cuneiform at Hattusa.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/linear-b.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Term · c. 1450–1200 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Linear B</span><span class="path-step-why">The Mycenaean palaces' syllabic script, adapted from Linear A, listed goods, workers and offerings; Michael Ventris showed in 1952 that it was Greek.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/kingdom-of-ugarit"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tale-of-aqhat.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · 1800 BCE – 1185 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Kingdom of Ugarit</span><span class="path-step-why">Its scribes pressed an alphabet of 30 cuneiform signs into clay, and the fire that destroyed the city baked their tablets hard.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/edicts-of-ashoka"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edicts-of-ashoka.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Work</span><span class="path-step-name">Edicts of Ashoka</span><span class="path-step-why">The Maurya emperor's inscriptions on rocks and pillars, of the third century BCE, are the earliest substantial written records of Indian history.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Work · 196 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Rosetta Stone</span><span class="path-step-why">One decree of 196 BCE in hieroglyphs, Demotic and Greek: its parallel texts let Champollion decipher hieroglyphs in 1822.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Person · 9th century</span><span class="path-step-name">Cyril and Methodius</span><span class="path-step-why">Byzantine brothers who created the Glagolitic alphabet in the ninth century and translated the liturgy into Old Church Slavonic.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/movable-type"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/movable-type.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Term</span><span class="path-step-name">Movable type</span><span class="path-step-why">Bi Sheng's reusable ceramic characters of the eleventh century, about four centuries before Gutenberg's metal type.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/hangul"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hangul.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Term · 1443</span><span class="path-step-name">Hangul</span><span class="path-step-why">An alphabet made in 1443 under King Sejong and promulgated as 'the proper sounds for instructing the people', long overshadowed by classical Chinese.</span></span></a></li></ol>
